        "body": "Across 55 tracked repositories, cumulative stars rose by roughly 29,000 in the 18 days ending August 31, 2026, a gain of about 1,600 stars per day, with no pause on weekends or other visible interruption.\n\nThe 15 most-starred projects span the full stack of open AI software: training frameworks, inference engines, agent orchestration, fine-tuning tools, and model-serving layers. The top repo by stars is tensorflow/tensorflow at just under 200,000, and the list includes both decade-old foundations like pytorch/pytorch and keras-team/keras and newer inference-focused projects like vllm-project/vllm and ggml-org/llama.cpp. The 47 repositories pushed in the last 7 days and 49 in the last 30 days indicate that active development is broadly distributed rather than concentrated in one or two projects.\n\nThe steady daily increment in aggregate stars suggests sustained and growing public interest, but stars measure attention, not compute consumed or code quality. The data does not include contributor counts, commit frequency, or issue velocity, so it cannot say whether development effort is accelerating or holding steady. Nothing here connects directly to power or physical infrastructure, but the prominence of inference-optimization projects, particularly vllm-project/vllm, ggml-org/llama.cpp, and unslothai/unsloth, reflects a community preoccupied with getting more output from a fixed hardware budget, which is consistent with compute remaining a binding constraint.",
